  The dangling direction prompt bug saga continues.

Previous discussion can be found here:

<http://groups.google.com/group/rec.games.roguelike.nethack/browse_thread/thread/143e5529989dd036/3760d9d22b8a4d13?lnk=st&q=group%3Arec.games.roguelike.nethack+insubject%3Adangling+author%3AJove&rnum=2&hl=en#3760d9d22b8a4d13>


Previous patch can be found here:

<http://groups.google.com/group/rec.games.roguelike.nethack/browse_thread/thread/4be811ac4beb385b/effdc696dbfa1746?lnk=st&q=group%3Arec.games.roguelike.nethack+insubject%3Adangling+author%3AJove&rnum=1&hl=en#effdc696dbfa1746>


  The bug consists of the "In what direction?" prompt remaining 
on screen when the program is no longer expecting input in 
response to the prompt.

  There is now a fix to one aspect of the bug reported on www.nethack.org:

From <http://www.nethack.org/cgi-bin/bugsearch-.pl>

>C343-96
>3.4.3
>fixed
>If player aborts "In what direction?" question, prompt may remain displayed.


  Unfortunately, the prompt will also remain displayed if the 
result of the action (which is prompting for a direction) does 
not result in a message.  

  E.g.

  - Throwing an object which hits nothing.
  - Throwing an object the results of which are unobserved by the player.
  - Casting a spell the results of which are unobserved by the player.
  - etc.



  In short, any action that prompts for a direction may result in
the prompt remaining on screen after that action is finished.  
The prompt remains on screen any time it is not overwritten by a 
message describing the result of the action.

  If there is no result of the action, the prompt remains.
  If the result of the action is unobserved by the player (due 
   to blindness, distance, etc.) the prompt remains on screen.


  Tracing decision trees through the code to determine where the
prompt needs to be explicitly cleared is problematical.  Thanks 
to Janis Papanagnou for pointing this out:

<http://groups.google.com/group/rec.games.roguelike.nethack/msg/2a84c3a7ef794b2c?dmode=source&hl=en>


  A much simpler fix is therefore possible: clear the prompt 
after its input has been received.  (This is done in multiple
other places in the code already.  So this fix seems to be in
line with current coding practice for NetHack.)

  The essential function of the patch is to add the line:

        clear_nhwindow(WIN_MESSAGE);

immediately after the lines:

            dirsym = yn_function ((s && *s != '^') ? s : "In what direction?",
                                        (char *)0, '\0');


in src\cmd.c, approximate line 2093.


C:\MyApps\Games\Nethack\Source>diff -NurbBX ignore.txt "pristine\src" "Prompt_Fix_New\src"


============================ BEGIN PATCH ==================================
diff -NurbBX ignore.txt pristine\src/cmd.c Prompt_Fix_New\src/cmd.c
--- pristine\src/cmd.c  2003-12-07 18:39:12.000000000 -0600
+++ Prompt_Fix_New\src/cmd.c    2006-01-04 14:46:24.000000000 -0600
@@ -5,6 +5,7 @@
 #include "hack.h"
 #include "func_tab.h"
 /* #define DEBUG */    /* uncomment for debugging */
+#define PROMPT_FIX

 /*
  * Some systems may have getchar() return EOF for various reasons, and
@@ -2088,6 +2089,13 @@
 #endif
            dirsym = yn_function ((s && *s != '^') ? s : "In what direction?",
                                        (char *)0, '\0');
+
+#ifdef PROMPT_FIX
+        clear_nhwindow(WIN_MESSAGE);
+#endif
+
+
+
 #ifdef REDO
        savech(dirsym);
 #endif
============================ END PATCH ==================================


